############################################################
Section::Begin("");

BEGIN_PGML
Now, set [`T_3(0)=f(0)\text{,}`] [`T_3'(0)=f'(0)\text{,}`] [`T_3''(0)=f''(0)\text{,}`] and [`T_3'''(0)=f'''(0)\text{.}`]

This implies [`c_0={}`][_]{'0'}{10}, [`c_1={}`][_]{'1'}{10}, [`c_2={}`][_]{'0'}{10}, and [`c_3={}`][_]{$f}{10}.

Putting it all together, what is the resulting formula for [`T_3(x)\text{?}`]

[`T_3(x)={}`][_]{$T3}{20}

END_PGML

Section::End();

############################################################
# Section
############################################################
Section::Begin("");

BEGIN_PGML
In our work so far, we've seen that raising the degree of polynomial improves the approximation of the original function [`f(x)\text{.}`]  If we decided we wanted to find a degree [`9`] approximation to [`f(x) = \sin(x)\text{,}`] we'd need to find a polynomial whose value and first [`9`] derivatives match those of the sine function at [`x = 0\text{.}`]

Fill in the blanks below to determine the next six derivatives of [`f(x) = \sin(x)\text{,}`] up to [`f^{(9)}(x)\text{.}`]

>> [@DataTable(
  [
    [
     PGML('[`f(x)=`]'),
     PGML('[`\sin(x)`]'),
     PGML('[`f^{(5)}(x)=`]'),
     PGML('[_]{$c}{10}'),
    ],
    [
     PGML(q"[`f'(x)=`]"),
     PGML('[`\cos(x)`]'),
     PGML('[`f^{(6)}(x)=`]'),
     PGML('[_]{-$s}{10}'),
    ],
    [
     PGML(q"[`f''(x)=`]"),
     PGML('[`-\sin(x)`]'),
     PGML('[`f^{(7)}(x)=`]'),
     PGML('[_]{-$c}{10}'),
    ],
    [
     PGML(q"[`f'''(x)=`]"),
     PGML('[`-\cos(x)`]'),
     PGML('[`f^{(8)}(x)=`]'),
     PGML('[_]{$s}{10}'),
    ],
    [
     PGML('[`f^{(4)}(x)=`]'),
     PGML('[_]{$s}{10}'),
     PGML('[`f^{(9)}(x)=`]'),
     PGML('[_]{$c}{10}'),
    ],
  ],
      align => 'rl|rl',
      valign => 'middle',);@]* <<

What is the value of [`f^{(n)}(0)`] (the nth derivative of [`f`] evaluated at [`x = 0`]) when [`n`] is even? [_]{$popup2}{5}

What is the value of [`f^{(n)}(0)`] (the nth derivative of [`f`] evaluated at [`x = 0`]) when [`n`] is odd? [_]{$popup1}{5}

END_PGML

Section::End();

